const EXISTING_SUMMARY = [
  "Colorectal cancer is the disease where screening works best: removing adenomas at colonoscopy prevents cancer, stool and blood tests catch it early, and localised disease is cured by surgery. It is the third most common cancer worldwide (about 1.9 million cases a year) and, because many are still found late, the second most common cause of cancer death (900,000 a year). Most cases arise through the adenoma-carcinoma sequence driven by APC loss, KRAS mutation, and TP53 loss (chromosomal instability); about 15% arise through mismatch-repair deficiency (dMMR/MSI-high), either sporadically via MLH1 methylation or through Lynch syndrome. Incidence is rising sharply in adults under 50 for reasons that remain unexplained.",
];

